"executive_summary": "This study investigates the role of 3D genome alterations in the pathogenesis and prognosis of T-lineage acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). By integrating Hi-C and RNA-seq data from 18 newly diagnosed T-ALL patients and 4 healthy controls, the authors demonstrate that 3D genome organization hierarchically classifies T-ALL subtypes according to T cell differentiation trajectories. The analysis reveals that 3D genome alterations are associated with nearly 45% of upregulated genes in T-ALL. A key finding is the identification of 34 previously unrecognized translocations in noncoding regions and 44 new translocation-mediated loops. These structural changes facilitate enhancer hijacking, particularly of the HOXA cluster, leading to ectopic expression of specific transcription factors. The study further dissects the heterogeneity of HOXA-positive T-ALLs, distinguishing between 3'HOXA+ and 5'HOXA+ subgroups based on TAD boundary shifts and enhancer interactions. Clinically, the authors show that ectopic expression of HOXA11-A13, but not other HOXA genes, is associated with immature phenotypes and poor survival outcomes. The findings suggest that 3D genome rewiring, specifically through translocation-mediated enhancer hijacking, drives the specific pathogenic gene expression profiles of T-ALL subgroups. Furthermore, the association of HOXA11-A13 positivity with JAK-STAT pathway mutations and poor prognosis suggests that anti-JAK-STAT inhibitors may benefit this high-risk patient group. This work provides a mechanistic framework linking chromatin architecture to oncogene activation and clinical outcomes in T-ALL.",
